# DNA-AND content · stars
# researched: Wikipedia "Stellar classification" (Morgan–Keenan system, luminosity classes, extended classes C, S, L, T, Y, D)
# Real seeds for the star generator, plus its rules.
# Values are typical ranges from real astronomy. Spec: docs/CONTENT-FORMAT.md

# ── Main-sequence stars (the seven classic types) ──

@star o-type
name: O-type blue star
real: yes
color: blue
temp-k: 30000..50000
mass-sol: 16..90
life-years: 1e6..1e7
share-of-stars: rarest
death: supernova
tags: hot, bright, short-lived, ultraviolet

@star b-type
name: B-type blue-white star
real: yes
color: blue-white
temp-k: 10000..30000
mass-sol: 2.1..16
life-years: 1e7..3e8
share-of-stars: rare
death: supernova at the heavy end, white dwarf at the light end
tags: hot, bright

@star a-type
name: A-type white star
real: yes
color: white
temp-k: 7500..10000
mass-sol: 1.4..2.1
life-years: 4e8..3e9
share-of-stars: uncommon
death: white dwarf
example: Sirius A
tags: bright

@star f-type
name: F-type yellow-white star
real: yes
color: yellow-white
temp-k: 6000..7500
mass-sol: 1.04..1.4
life-years: 3e9..7e9
share-of-stars: uncommon
death: white dwarf
tags: stable, life-friendly

@star g-type
name: G-type yellow star
real: yes
color: yellow
temp-k: 5200..6000
mass-sol: 0.8..1.04
life-years: 1e10
share-of-stars: common
death: red giant, then white dwarf
example: the Sun
tags: stable, life-friendly

@star k-type
name: K-type orange star
real: yes
color: orange
temp-k: 3700..5200
mass-sol: 0.45..0.8
life-years: 1.5e10..4.5e10
share-of-stars: common
death: white dwarf
tags: stable, long-lived, life-friendly

@star m-type
name: M-type red dwarf
real: yes
color: red
temp-k: 2400..3700
mass-sol: 0.08..0.45
life-years: 1e11..1e13
share-of-stars: most common
death: white dwarf, far in the future
example: Proxima Centauri
tags: dim, long-lived, flares

# ── Giants and supergiants ──

@star red-giant
name: Red giant
real: yes
color: red-orange
stage: late life of a Sun-like star
tags: swollen, cool surface

@star red-supergiant
name: Red supergiant
real: yes
color: red
stage: late life of a heavy star
example: Betelgeuse
death: supernova
tags: huge, cool surface

@star blue-supergiant
name: Blue supergiant
real: yes
color: blue
example: Rigel
death: supernova
tags: huge, very bright

@star wolf-rayet
name: Wolf-Rayet star
real: yes
color: blue
stage: heavy star blowing off its outer layers
tags: very hot, strong winds

# ── Young and failed stars ──

@star protostar
name: Protostar
real: yes
stage: a star still forming inside a gas cloud
tags: young, hidden in dust

@star t-tauri
name: T Tauri star
real: yes
stage: a young Sun-like star, not yet steady
tags: young, flaring, variable

@star brown-dwarf
name: Brown dwarf
real: yes
mass-jupiter: 13..80
stage: too light to burn like a true star
tags: dim, failed star

# ── Dead and strange stars ──

@star white-dwarf
name: White dwarf
real: yes
color: white
stage: the leftover core of a Sun-like star
tags: small, dense, cooling

@star neutron-star
name: Neutron star
real: yes
stage: the crushed core left by a supernova
tags: tiny, extremely dense

@star pulsar
name: Pulsar
real: yes
stage: a spinning neutron star sweeping beams through space
tags: spinning, beams, radiation

@star magnetar
name: Magnetar
real: yes
stage: a neutron star with an enormous magnetic field
tags: magnetic, star-quakes

@star black-hole
name: Stellar black hole
real: yes
stage: the collapsed core of a very heavy star
tags: no light, swallows matter

@star cepheid
name: Cepheid variable
real: yes
stage: a giant that swells and shrinks on a steady beat
tags: pulsing, variable

# ── Luminosity classes (how bright for its type) ──

@class hypergiant
name: Hypergiant (class 0 / Ia+)
real: yes
says: the brightest, heaviest stars, losing mass in huge winds
example: Eta Carinae

@class supergiant
name: Supergiant (class I)
real: yes
says: enormous, very bright stars near the end of their lives

@class bright-giant
name: Bright giant (class II)
real: yes
says: between giants and supergiants

@class giant
name: Giant (class III)
real: yes
example: Arcturus

@class subgiant
name: Subgiant (class IV)
real: yes
says: a star just starting to swell into a giant

@class main-sequence
name: Main-sequence star (class V)
real: yes
says: a star steadily burning hydrogen, like the Sun

@class subdwarf
name: Subdwarf (class VI)
real: yes
says: dimmer than a main-sequence star of the same color

# ── Extra star types ──

@star carbon-star
name: Carbon star (class C)
real: yes
color: deep red
says: a cool giant whose air is rich in carbon

@star s-type
name: S-type star
real: yes
says: a cool giant between ordinary red giants and carbon stars

@star l-dwarf
name: L-type brown dwarf
real: yes
says: the warmest brown dwarfs, with metal-hydride clouds

@star t-dwarf
name: T-type brown dwarf
real: yes
says: cooler brown dwarfs with methane in their air

@star y-dwarf
name: Y-type brown dwarf
real: yes
says: the coldest brown dwarfs, barely warmer than a planet

@star rr-lyrae
name: RR Lyrae variable
real: yes
says: an old pulsing star that brightens and dims in less than a day

@star mira
name: Mira variable
real: yes
says: a pulsing red giant that brightens and dims over months

# ── Generator rules ──

@rule star-color
real: rule
says: color follows surface temperature, from red (coolest) to blue (hottest)

@rule star-life
real: rule
says: heavier stars burn hotter and die sooner, lighter stars burn cooler and live longer

@rule star-pick
real: rule
says: when a galaxy is generated, red dwarfs are by far the most common and blue stars the rarest

@rule star-vary
real: rule
says: every generated star picks its values from inside its type's ranges, from the world seed

@rule star-name
real: rule
says: only our own star is called the Sun; every other star is a star
